# iterthink 2.0

A local-first workspace for project work with AI. One line to start any task;
documents, chats and ad-hoc apps emerge from it; open files hold the results;
every change, human or AI, passes review and lands in a sealed log.
Change detection and impact judgment (1.x) become building blocks inside it.

2.0 replaces the Flet UI with a local web UI in a native window. The Python core
(compare, services, persistence, ai, db) stays and becomes the single source of
logic for the UI, agents and future sync. This file supersedes `.cursorrules`
where they conflict (notably "NO JavaScript, NO HTML/CSS").

# Role & tone
- Act as a senior software engineer. Concise, technical, direct.
- Root cause first, then the fix as code or diff. No summaries, no filler.
- Distinguish source code from runtime/data state. Flag seed files
  (`defaults/*.yaml`) as seeds immediately.

# Principles
- Local-first. No account. Source files stay where they are: iterthink adapts
  to the user's existing folders and never moves, renames or reorganizes them.
  iterthink's own data travels with the project in `<project>/.iterthink/`.
- No lock-in. Source files stay in open formats and open without iterthink.
- Track decisions, not files. Every change ends in a verdict: a human decision,
  or an automatic one under a rule the user set in advance (`rules.yaml`
  `direct`), recorded as "auto · rule <id>".
- Nothing is applied without the user's decision. AI, agents and apps propose.
- One core, many surfaces. UI, ad-hoc apps (bridge), MCP and CLI call the same
  registry of core functions (`services/bridge.py`).
- Keyboard: `Mod` = Ctrl on Windows/Linux, Cmd on macOS. All shortcuts below
  use `Mod` and are configurable.
- Plain vocabulary in the UI: version, change, proposal, accept, reject, restore,
  history. Never Git terms.

# Folder model
- A workspace is any folder the user points iterthink at (local disk, external
  drive, network share). Several workspaces are allowed; the list of known
  workspaces is machine state. No fixed `Documents/iterthink` location.
- Each first-level subfolder is a project = one `Project` row. Deeper
  subfolders are plain folders inside the project. A user can also mark any
  single folder as a project without a workspace around it.
- The project is the unit of history, sealed log chain, RAG scope, checks
  context, sync and sharing. Never mix rows or RAG context across projects.
- `services/rag/project_scope.py` already scopes RAG by project: keep that
  boundary for every new feature (MCP tools take a `project` argument).

## Source files stay, iterthink files move
- iterthink never moves, renames, restructures or deletes source files on its
  own. It writes to a source file only when the user accepts a change to it.
  Moving files is a user action (or an accepted proposal), never automatic.
- Everything iterthink creates for a project lives in `<project>/.iterthink/`
  and moves with the folder (copy, rename, archive, hand over, sync):

```
<project>/
  ...source files, untouched...
  .iterthink/
    log/
      <machine-id>.jsonl  # append-only event log, one file per machine (the truth)
    versions/         # content-addressed snapshots (sha256) of tracked files
    apps/             # ad-hoc apps (+ .archive/)
    tasks/            # task files
    chats/            # chat files
    settings/         # project overrides: prompts, checks, rules, agents
    exports/          # delivery exports (DOCX / XLSX / PDF)
```

- User-facing outputs the user asks for (a new document, a schedule CSV) are
  written where the user says, default the project root, and are then normal
  source files.
- Workspace-level `.iterthink/` holds only workspace defaults (settings
  inheritance), no project data.
- Machine-level data stays in the OS app directory (`~/.config/iterthink`,
  `~/Library/Application Support/iterthink`, `%APPDATA%\iterthink`): known
  workspaces, downloaded models (embeddings, reranker, STT), UI state, and the
  per-project index databases (`index/<project-id>.db`), which are caches
  rebuilt from the project's files. Secrets stay in the OS keyring. Nothing
  there is the only copy of anything.
- On Windows, set the hidden attribute on `.iterthink/`.
- Opening a moved or copied project just works: paths inside `.iterthink/`
  are relative to the project root. A copied project gets a new project id
  on first open (asked: "copy or same project?") so chains do not fork silently.
- Handover / archive = the project folder as is. Audit export adds a
  verification script and a readable report into `.iterthink/exports/`.
- Files directly in the workspace folder (outside any project) are not
  tracked. The explorer shows them as untracked and offers "make this folder a
  project" or "ignore"; it never moves them. A new first-level subfolder
  prompts "Make project".
- Moving a file between projects (done by the user) is recorded as removed in
  one and added in the other, with a reference to the source.

## Shared folders (network drives, NAS, synced folders)
- No database lives on the share, so there is no SQLite locking problem.
  Several machines can open the same project at the same time.
- Each machine appends only to its own `log/<machine-id>.jsonl`; no two
  writers ever touch the same file. Other machines' logs are read, tailed
  and merged into the local index (file watcher + periodic rescan, since
  network file systems drop change notifications).
- Version snapshots are content-addressed and written once (write to a
  temp name, then rename), so concurrent writers cannot corrupt them.
- Task, chat and app files: one writer at a time per file via a short-lived
  claim (`<file>.claim` with machine, user, heartbeat). A second editor gets
  read-only with a banner; stale claims (no heartbeat for 2 min) can be taken
  over, logged as an audit event.
- Conflicting decisions (two machines accept different candidates for the same
  baseline) are detected when logs merge and surface in review as a conflict,
  like competing agent proposals. Never resolved silently.
- Detect cloud-sync folders (OneDrive, Dropbox, iCloud): logs and snapshots
  are safe there; warn that simultaneous edits of the same source file on two
  machines produce sync-client conflict copies, which iterthink then shows as
  separate candidates.

# Data model
Follows the pragmaticBIM data contract (schema.pragmaticbim.ch, versioned;
`contract/` holds the enums in use). One content table, one relations table.
No feature gets its own entity table.

## Storage: files are the truth, SQLite is the index
- Source of truth = files in the project: source files, `versions/`
  snapshots, `log/*.jsonl`, task / chat / app / settings files.
- The event log records every state change as one JSON line: content version
  created, relation added, change detected, verdict given, audit event.
  Canonical JSON (sorted keys, UTF-8, no floats for ids), schema-versioned
  with the contract version.

```json
{"seq":1042,"ts":"2026-10-23T05:38:12Z","machine":"m-7f3a","actor":"user:simon","type":"verdict","project":"p-19c2","payload":{"change_id":"c-88e1","verdict":"rejected","reason":"EI 90 required"},"contract":"1.0.8","prev_hash":"…","hash":"…"}
```

- Every write goes through `services/event_log.py`: append the event to the
  local machine's log, fsync, then apply it to the index. Never write to the
  index directly; never write a log line from anywhere else.
- The SQLite database (`content`, `content_relations`, `content_changes`,
  `audit_events`, FTS5, embeddings, analysis) is a projection, stored
  machine-local, rebuilt by `services/indexer.py` from the logs and files.
  It remembers per log file the last applied offset and hash, so updates are
  incremental; a full rebuild is always possible and must give the same result.
- Schema changes to the index = bump index version and rebuild. Alembic stays
  only for the index schema, never as migration of user data.
- Merge order across machine logs: by `ts`, then `machine`, then `seq`;
  events are idempotent (`event id` = hash), so replays are safe.
- Projection tables below describe the index; their meaning is defined by the
  events that build them.

## Tables (index projection)

- `content`: every thing is a row, distinguished by `content_kind` /
  `canonical_type`, versioned by `lineage_id` + `version_no` + `is_latest`.
  Documents, plans, models, CSV tables, chats, ad-hoc apps, exports and
  candidate versions are all content rows.
- `content_relations`: every link is a row (versioned like content):
  derived_from (export → source version), proposes (candidate → baseline),
  produced_by (output → app), imported_from (working copy → original),
  references (chat → document), contains (project → content).
- Satellite tables stay as in 1.x: `files` + `content_file_links` (storage),
  `content_geometries` (geometry representations).
- Change records (`content_changes`, contract `Change` / `PropertyChange` /
  `GeometryChange`) and `audit_events` are audit records, not entities:
  outside the content graph; projections of the append-only, hash-chained log.
- Derived data (paragraph analysis, embeddings, annotations, comments,
  token usage) may keep its own tables; it is rebuildable or user input, never
  a second home for content.
- Mapping of 2.0 concepts:
  - proposal / candidate → content row with `status = candidate` +
    `proposes` relation to the baseline version; accept sets it latest
  - ad-hoc app → content row (artifact kind `app`), code as linked file,
    manifest in `attributes`; kept / discarded via `status`
  - chat → content row (artifact kind `text_document`), file in `.iterthink/chats/`
  - task → content row (kind `task`), file in `.iterthink/tasks/`; `depends_on` and
    `produced_by` (proposal → task) as relations
  - IFC element changes → `content_changes` with GlobalId as subject;
    elements themselves are not content rows unless a workflow needs them
- New kinds, statuses or relation types are added to the contract first
  (versioned enum), then used. No ad-hoc strings in code.

# What stays (core, reused as-is)
- `compare/` — alignment (TF-IDF + rapidfuzz), word diff, embedding cosine bands,
  LLM tie-break, slot kinds: stable, refined, modified, rephrased, added, removed.
  `paragraph_align.word_diff_html` already returns `<ins>`/`<del>` HTML: use it
  directly in templates.
- `services/` — import (PDF, DOCX, OCR), plan PDF text diff (bbox IoU), impact
  analysis runner, checks runner, RAG (sqlite-vec, fastembed), exports.
- `persistence/`, `db/`, `alembic/` — SQLAlchemy models and queries stay as
  the index schema (content lineage with `version_no` / `is_latest`,
  disk-state tracking by sha256). Their writes are rerouted through
  `services/event_log.py`; reads are unchanged. Migration: an importer turns
  an existing 1.x `store_db` into log events and snapshots once.
- `contract/` — the change contract: every change is a `ContentChange` row with
  `change_class` (PropertyChange | GeometryChange), `affected_subject_id`,
  `property_path`, `from_value`, `to_value`, `intent_verdict`, `change_source`.
  All formats, including IFC, map onto this contract. Do not invent parallel
  change models.
- `ai/` — `llm_router` (Ollama local default; company and cloud tiers via
  OpenAI-compatible, Anthropic, Gemini), privacy shield (regex + LLM redaction with
  placeholder reinject before company/cloud calls), token usage and cost tracking,
  local embeddings, OCR, keyring-backed secrets.
- Config pattern: bundled `defaults/*.yaml` seed runtime copies in the
  workspace `.iterthink/settings/` (overridable per project)
  (`prompts.yaml`, `checks.yaml`, `impact_checks.yaml`, `privacy_shield.yaml`,
  `config.yaml`); on upgrade, new defaults merge in and changed defaults go through
  a review dialog. Keep this pattern for every new config file.

# What changes
- Remove: Flet (`studio/`, `app_entry.py`, `compare/diff_card.py` flet spans).
- Add: `web/` (FastAPI, Jinja, HTMX, static), `desktop.py` (pywebview), `mcp/`.
- Core must not import UI code. `compare/diff_card.py` currently imports flet:
  split it into a pure judge module (stays in `compare/`) and drop the span builder.
  CI check: `grep -r "import flet" iterthink/` outside `studio/` fails the build.

# Tech stack
- Python 3.11+, `uv`, existing dependencies minus `flet`
- API and pages: FastAPI + Uvicorn on 127.0.0.1, random port, token auth
- Desktop shell: pywebview (WebView2 / WebKit / WebKitGTK), no browser controls
- Frontend: plain HTML, no build step
  - Jinja2 templates rendered server-side
  - HTMX for interactions; server holds state, returns HTML fragments
  - Vanilla JS ES modules only where needed, as custom elements
  - `// @ts-check` + JSDoc types; no TypeScript compile, no Node, no bundler
  - One `app.css` with custom properties; third-party JS vendored in
    `static/vendor/`, loaded via import map, works offline
- Live updates: Server-Sent Events (HTMX `sse` extension) for analysis progress,
  LLM streaming, file changes on disk
- IFC: IfcOpenShell for parsing, diff by GlobalId, GLB export per version and
  a diff GLB per version pair; Three.js (vendored) for the 3D view
- PDF plans: keep pypdfium2 rendering; overlays as positioned HTML/SVG over the
  page image, using existing normalized bbox data
- Tables: daff (diff), openpyxl (xlsx import and export)
- Voice: faster-whisper (local speech-to-text), sounddevice (audio capture)
- Agents: MCP server (official Python SDK) on the same core
- Quality: ruff, mypy (strict on new modules and `compare/`), pytest (keep the
  existing suite green)

