# The line (start screen and universal entry)
White screen, one pulsing dot, one line: "What do you want to do?"
The mode emerges from what the user does; the user never picks a mode first.

## Core rule
- The AI never reacts while the user types. No suggestions, no autocomplete,
  no "make this a table?" hints. It acts only when invited.
- Invitation = Enter in the line and in chat; Mod+Enter wherever Enter
  already has a job (document, app).
- Surfaces open full width with nothing around them. Explorer, history,
  checks and review open as panels on demand, never by default. The 1.x
  three-column layout is gone.

## First Enter decides the mode
`services/intent.py` classifies the line (via `llm_router`, small local model
first) and returns a mode plus a plan. The AI builds the surface; it does not
fill it unless asked.

| Line example                              | Mode     | Result                                             |
|-------------------------------------------|----------|----------------------------------------------------|
| "Fire protection concept, Schulhaus Basel"| document | new .md with that title, cursor ready, no AI text  |
| "Which doors are EI 30?"                  | chat     | chat with the answer                               |
| "Compare spec v6 and v7"                  | review   | review screen for the two versions                 |
| "Door schedule from the model"            | app      | ad-hoc app (table) generated for the task          |
| file dropped on the screen                | review   | what changed since the last version, or import     |
| "Have an agent check all specs"           | task     | task files in Backlog with a proposed split        |

Low confidence → do not guess silently: build the most likely surface and show
one quiet line "Opened as document · switch" with the alternatives.

## Enter by mode
| Mode     | Enter                          | Mod+Enter                           |
|----------|--------------------------------|-------------------------------------|
| line     | hand over, decide mode         | same as Enter                       |
| document | new paragraph                  | AI on current paragraph / selection |
| chat     | send                           | send                                |
| app      | confirm current field / action | ask the AI to change the app        |

Shift+Enter is a line break in line and chat.

## Recall and state
- Mod+K, or `/` at the start of an empty paragraph, brings the line back from
  any mode. It is scoped: inside a document it acts on that document; on the
  start screen on the project.
- The dot carries state: steady = idle, pulsing = something waits for review.
  Clicking it opens the review queue.
- Typing a project name, a file name or "continue" in the line reopens work.
- The line needs a project. If none is active, the first answer places the
  work in one or creates it (folder model applies).

## Voice input
Voice is another way to type, not another mode. Same core rule: speech becomes
text in the line (or document, or chat); nothing happens until Enter.

- Trigger: hold the dot, or hold a configurable key, to speak; release to
  stop. Default key: Mod+Shift+Space. Never Ctrl+Space: it switches input
  sources on macOS and IBus on Linux. The dot shows state: listening,
  transcribing. Hands-free toggle optional.
- Speech-to-text runs locally by default: faster-whisper (CTranslate2) in
  `ai/local_stt.py`, model downloaded on first use into the machine app directory like the
  embedding model. Model size selectable in Settings (small default).
- Audio capture in Python (`sounddevice`) in `services/dictation.py`, not in
  the WebView, so behaviour is identical on WebView2, WebKit and WebKitGTK.
  macOS: add the microphone usage description and entitlement to packaging.
- Partial transcripts stream into the active input via SSE; the user can edit
  the text before pressing Enter.
- By mode:
  - line: transcript fills the line; Enter hands over as usual
  - document: dictation inserts at the cursor; Mod+Enter invites the AI
  - chat: transcript fills the input; Enter sends
  - app: transcript fills the focused field
- Languages: German, English, French, Italian; language auto-detected or
  fixed in Settings. Swiss German is transcribed to Standard German; test with
  real project dictation before promising more.
- Privacy: audio is never stored and never leaves the machine by default.
  A cloud STT tier is opt-in per workspace with an explicit warning, since the
  privacy shield cannot redact audio. Only the transcript is processed further,
  and it passes the privacy shield like typed text.
- Audit: input source (typed / voice) is recorded with the AI action it led to.
  No audio, no confidence scores in the log.

## Everything is a file
- Documents: `.md` in the project.
- Chats: `.md` in `<project>/.iterthink/chats/`, one file per conversation, versioned
  like any document. A chat can be turned into a document and back.
- Ad-hoc app outputs (tables, schedules): CSV / MD / JSON files in the project.
- Every transformation (chat → document, app → table file) is a new version.
  Nothing is lost; a misread mode costs one step.

## Ad-hoc apps
- Generated as one self-contained HTML file (+ optional JS module) by the AI
  for the task in the line.
- Stored in `<project>/.iterthink/apps/<slug>/index.html` with a
  `manifest.yaml` (purpose, inputs, created by, model, prompt).
- Rendered in a sandboxed iframe: `sandbox="allow-scripts"` without
  `allow-same-origin`, own CSP, no network. It talks to iterthink only through
  `postMessage` with a narrow bridge (`static/js/app-bridge.js`, server side
  `services/bridge.py`). Every call runs in core, scoped to the current project.
  - No access to settings, secrets, other projects or the LLM router directly;
    AI blocks go through the router with privacy shield and token logging.
  - The frame has no token and makes no requests to the API. The parent page
    receives bridge messages, checks them against the allowlist and calls the
    API with its token.
  - Delivery: the app HTML is loaded via `srcdoc` with its own CSP meta tag
    (`default-src 'none'; script-src 'unsafe-inline' <vendor origin>;
    style-src 'unsafe-inline'; img-src data: blob:`). Inline script is allowed
    inside the frame only; the app shell keeps its strict CSP. Vendor modules
    are served with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: null` so module scripts load
    from the frame's opaque origin.

## Formats: work open, deliver closed
- Working files and app outputs are open formats only: CSV (tables),
  Markdown (text), JSON (structured data), GLB (geometry previews).
- DOCX, XLSX and PDF are delivery exports, generated from an open file on
  request, stored under `<project>/.iterthink/exports/` unless the user picks a location, never edited, never the source.
  Each export records which version of which source it came from.
- Incoming DOCX, XLSX and PDF from others are inputs: the original is kept
  as received (versioned, compared as-is) and imported into an open working
  copy (MD / CSV) when the user works on it. Plans (PDF) stay PDF.

## Building blocks (bridge functions)
Ad-hoc apps compose these. The AI writes glue and layout, not logic.
Prefer an existing block over generated code; a missing block is a backlog
item for core, not something an app reimplements.

| Block     | Bridge functions                                              | Core module (exists / new)                         |
|-----------|---------------------------------------------------------------|----------------------------------------------------|
| Read      | `listFiles(glob)`, `readFile(path, version?)`, `history(path)` | persistence (exists)                               |
| Import    | `importPdf(path)`, `importDocx(path)`, `ocr(path)`, `planText(path)` | document_import, ocr_import, plan_text_extract (exists) |
| Compare   | `compare(path, base, candidate)` → ContentChange rows + render payload | compare/, plan_text_diff, pdf_visual_diff (exists) |
| Judge     | `runCheck(check_id, target)`, `impact(target)`                | checks_runner, impact_analysis_runner (exists)     |
| Find      | `search(query, kind?, in_history?, k?)` — hybrid, see Search  | services/rag (exists), services/search (new)       |
| Annotate  | `annotatePlan(path, page, pin)`, `comment(path, paragraph, text)` | plan_pdf_annotations, paragraph_user_comments (exists) |
| Model     | `queryIfc(path, query)`, `ifcProperties(path, guid)`, `ifcGlb(path, version?)` | engine_ifc (new)                     |
| Table     | `readTable(path)`, `diffTable(path, base, candidate)`         | engine_table (new)                                 |
| Language  | `spellcheck(text, lang?)`, `detectLanguage(text)`             | spell_resources (exists)                           |
| Write     | `propose(path, content, reason)` — CSV / MD / JSON only       | proposals → review (new)                           |
| Deliver   | `exportDocx(md_path, template?)`, `exportXlsx(csv_path)`, `exportPlanPdf(path)` | markdown_docx_export, plan_pdf_export (exists); xlsx (new) |

Writes always go through `propose`; nothing an app does reaches disk without
review. `Deliver` writes only to `.iterthink/exports/` (or the chosen location) and is logged.

## View components (for apps and screens alike)
Custom elements in `static/js/`, shared by built-in screens and ad-hoc apps:
`<md-preview>`, `<data-table>` (sort, filter, changed cells), `<plan-viewer>`,
`<ifc-viewer>` / `<ifc-diff>`, `<image-compare>`, `<diff-text>`, `<check-symbols>`,
`<simple-chart>` (bar / line from a CSV, new), `<form-fields>` (inputs that
write a JSON or CSV row via `propose`, new). Ported from `studio/` where a Flet
version exists.

## Examples
- Door schedule: `queryIfc(model, IfcDoor + Pset_DoorCommon)` → `<data-table>`
  → `propose("schedules/doors.csv")` → optional `exportXlsx`.
- Spec check: `importPdf(spec_v7)` → `compare(spec, v6, v7)` →
  `runCheck("project_impact")` → review.
- Fire rating audit: `queryIfc(model, FireRating)` + `search("EI 90")` →
  `<data-table>` of mismatches → `propose("checks/fire-ratings.csv")`.

## Lifecycle
- Ad hoc by default: removed from view when closed unless kept. "Keep"
  promotes it to a permanent tool listed in the explorer under the project.
- A discarded app's code and manifest move to `.iterthink/apps/.archive/`,
  because its proposals and audit entries reference it. Purged only with the
  project's audit export, never silently.
- Agent rules (`rules.yaml`) apply to apps exactly as to agents.
- App code changes (Mod+Enter in an app) are versions of the app file,
  reviewable like any other change.

## App builder
The builder is a guideline prompt plus task context plus validation.
Code lives in `services/adhoc_apps.py`; the prompt is a seed file.

### 1. Builder prompt (`defaults/app_builder.md`)
- Seeded into workspace `.iterthink/settings/` and merged on upgrade like `prompts.yaml`
  (new defaults added, changed defaults go through the review dialog).
  Overridable per workspace and project (folder model inheritance).
- Sections:
  1. Rules: one self-contained HTML file (+ optional module), no network,
     bridge calls only, writes only via `propose`, open formats only,
     existing blocks before own logic, plain vocabulary in the UI, theme
     tokens via CSS custom properties.
  2. Bridge reference: every function with signature, arguments, return shape
     and one example. **Generated** at startup from the bridge registry in
     `services/bridge.py` (docstrings + type hints), never written by hand,
     so the prompt cannot drift from the real API.
  3. Component reference: every custom element with attributes, events and
     one example. Generated from a registry in `static/js/components.json`.
  4. Example apps: three to five complete, working apps (door schedule, spec
     check, fire rating audit). Stored as real files in `defaults/app_examples/`
     and covered by tests, so examples always run against the current bridge.
- Output contract: the model returns JSON
  `{ "slug", "title", "purpose", "inputs": [...], "html": "...", "module": "..."? }`.
  Anything else is a failed attempt.

### 2. Task context (assembled per request)
- The user's line, verbatim.
- Project file list: paths, sizes, kinds. **No file contents.**
- Schemas where relevant, extracted locally: IFC classes and property sets
  present in the model, CSV headers, Markdown headings.
- Up to three kept apps whose `manifest.yaml` purpose matches the line (via
  local embeddings). The model adapts one instead of starting fresh.

### 3. Validation before the user sees the app
1. Parse the JSON contract.
2. Static check on the HTML / module:
   - no `fetch`, `XMLHttpRequest`, `WebSocket`, `import()` of remote URLs,
     `<script src>` or `<link href>` outside `/static/vendor/`
   - only allowlisted bridge function names
   - no `eval`, no `new Function`
   - size limit (e.g. 200 KB)
3. Run headless in the sandbox with the real bridge in dry-run mode
   (reads allowed, `propose` recorded, not applied). Capture console errors
   and uncaught exceptions.
4. On failure: send the error list back to the model with the previous output.
   Maximum 3 attempts, then show the user what failed in plain language.
5. On success: store under `.iterthink/apps/<slug>/` with `manifest.yaml`
   (purpose, inputs, created by, model, prompt version, attempts) and open it.

### Privacy model
- The model writes the app; it never sees the data. It receives the line,
  paths and schemas only. The app reads content at runtime through the bridge,
  locally.
- Therefore app building may use the cloud tier even for sensitive projects.
  Schemas still pass the privacy shield (names in property values, file names
  with persons or addresses are redacted).
- Settings: app builder tier selectable separately (local / company / cloud),
  default = strongest configured model, since code generation needs it.

### Logging
- Every build is an AI action in the audit trail: line, prompt version, model,
  attempts, validation results, kept or discarded.
- Build failures and user corrections are collected per prompt version to
  improve the builder prompt and to find missing blocks for core.

## Implementation
- `web/pages/line.py`, `templates/line.html`, `static/js/the-line.js`
  (`<the-line>`: input, Enter handling, recall shortcut, dot state via SSE).
- `services/intent.py` (classification + plan), `services/adhoc_apps.py`
  (prompt assembly, generate, validate, repair loop, store, promote, discard),
  `services/bridge.py` (bridge registry + reference generator),
  `defaults/app_builder.md`, `defaults/app_examples/`, `static/js/app-frame.js`
  (`<app-frame>`: sandboxed iframe + bridge).
- Intent classification is logged like every AI action (model, prompt id,
  chosen mode, user correction if any). Corrections improve the prompt, not a
  hidden model.

# Build order
The line is the product's front door, but it routes into surfaces that must
exist first. Build bottom-up, ship the line as soon as it has places to go.

1. **Foundation** — `web/` shell, pywebview window, token, SSE, theme tokens,
   `services/bridge.py` registry, `services/event_log.py` + `services/indexer.py`
   (files as truth, SQLite as index), `services/audit.py`, 1.x importer.
2. **Review** — the safety layer everything depends on. Change fragments for
   text, accept / reject / edit, verdicts into the chain.
3. **Document** — `<md-editor>` full width. Save creates a version; margin
   actions from `prompts.yaml` (Discuss / Edit / Evaluate) via Mod+Enter,
   streamed via SSE, results land in review.
4. **The line** — start screen, intent routing to document, chat and review.
   Chat as `.md` files. Voice input.
5. **Panels** — search (hybrid, text first; plans, CSV, IFC as their engines
   land), explorer (projects at top, untracked files flagged), history,
   checks and impact (`checks.yaml`, `impact_checks.yaml` with RAG context),
   opened on demand.
6. **Ad-hoc apps** — bridge to apps, `<app-frame>`, builder, validation,
   keep / discard. First components: `<data-table>`, `<md-preview>`.
7. **Tasks and agents** — task files, board, runner with built-in agent loop,
   MCP task tools for external agents, competing-proposal handling in review.
8. **Plans** — plan compare in review, `<plan-viewer>`.
9. **Tables** — `engine_table` (daff), `<simple-chart>`, `<form-fields>`.
10. **IFC** — `engine_ifc`: element changes as `ContentChange` rows
   (`affected_subject_id` = GlobalId, `property_path` = Pset.Property), metadata
   change table, GLB per version, diff GLB coloured by change category,
   `<ifc-diff>`, `queryIfc` block for apps.
11. **Settings** — models (incl. app builder and STT tier), paths, prompts with
    merge review, privacy shield, RAG, token cost, shortcuts.

Port one surface at a time. Flet and the web UI may coexist on a branch during
migration; delete `studio/` modules as their replacement ships.

# Tasks and agents (board)
Several agents work in parallel on tasks the user hands out. The board is a
built-in surface, not a generated app: it controls work and must behave the
same every time. Ad-hoc apps may embed `<task-board>` with a filter.

## Tasks are files
- One task = one Markdown file in `<project>/.iterthink/tasks/` with YAML frontmatter,
  indexed as a content row (kind `task`). Readable by any external agent or
  editor, versioned like any document, every status change is a version.

```markdown
---
id: t-0042
title: Check fire ratings in spec v7 against the model
status: ready            # backlog | ready | running | needs-input | review | done | rejected | cancelled
agent: spec-checker      # agent profile id, or "any"
inputs: [specs/fire-protection.md, models/building.ifc]
scope: [specs/**, checks/**]   # paths the agent may propose changes to
depends_on: [t-0040]
budget: { tokens: 200000, minutes: 20 }
created_by: user
---
Compare every fire rating in the spec with the IFC door and wall properties.
List mismatches as checks/fire-ratings.csv. Do not change the spec.
```

## Columns
Backlog → Ready → Running → Needs input → Review → Done (or Rejected /
Cancelled). Drag between Backlog and Ready is the user's handover; the system
moves the rest.
- Review: the task's proposals (`produced_by` relation) wait in review. The
  card links straight to them. Accepting all proposals moves the card to Done;
  rejecting moves it to Rejected with the reason.
- Needs input: the agent asked a question. The card shows it, the dot pulses,
  the user answers on the card (or via the line). Answers are appended to the
  task file.

## Agent profiles (`.iterthink/agents.yaml`, inherited)
```yaml
spec-checker:
  model_tier: cloud          # local | company | cloud
  blocks: [read, import, compare, judge, find, model, table, write]
  rules: default             # rules.yaml applies on top
  max_parallel: 2
```
- An agent can only use the bridge blocks its profile lists, only inside the
  task's `scope`, and only write via `propose`. `rules.yaml` still applies.

## Runner (`services/agent_runner.py`)
- Local orchestration: a worker pool picks Ready tasks whose dependencies are
  Done, up to the limits (global, per profile, per tier; local Ollama default
  1, cloud default 4).
- Built-in agents: a tool loop over `llm_router` with bridge functions as
  tools. External agents (Claude Code, other MCP clients) use MCP task tools:
  `list_tasks`, `claim_task`, `update_task(progress)`, `ask(question)`,
  `complete_task(summary)`. Both paths go through the same core.
- Progress streams to the card via SSE: current step, tokens used, elapsed.
- Budget exceeded → task pauses in Needs input with "budget reached".
- Cancel stops the agent; proposals already made stay in review.

## Parallel work and conflicts
- Every proposal records its baseline version. Agents never wait for each
  other; conflicts are detected, not prevented.
- Two proposals on the same baseline of the same file → review shows them as
  competing; accepting one marks the other outdated and offers "rebase":
  the agent reruns its change on the new version (new candidate).
- Optional soft claims: a task's `scope` is shown on other cards that overlap,
  so the user sees conflicts coming before handing out work.
- Later, with jj: each running task works in its own draft (branch).

## From the line
- "Have an agent …" / "check all … in parallel" → intent `task`: creates one
  or more task files in Backlog with a proposed split, shown on the board for
  the user to adjust and move to Ready. Nothing runs before the handover.

## Audit
- Task created, handed over, claimed, asked, budget hit, completed, cancelled
  are `audit_events` with actor `agent:<profile>` and the task id. Every AI
  action and proposal carries the task id.

# Search
Hybrid by default: the user never chooses between keyword and semantic.
Builds on `services/rag/` (embeddings, query variants, local reranker,
`/f` and `/p` prefixes), adds keyword search.

## Pipeline (`services/search.py`)
1. Parse: `"exact phrase"` → keyword only for that phrase; `/f` filename;
   `/p <project>` scope; filters `kind:` (doc, plan, model, table, chat, app),
   `in:history` (older versions and change records), `since:<date>`.
2. Keyword: SQLite FTS5 (built in, no new dependency), BM25 ranking, unicode
   tokenizer with diacritics folding, trigram index for partial codes
   ("EI 9", "1.04"). Exact tokens such as norms, fire classes, room numbers
   and GlobalIds always match here.
3. Semantic: existing chunk embeddings (sqlite-vec), query variants only via
   the local tier (or with privacy shield).
4. Fuse: Reciprocal Rank Fusion of both lists (k = 60), then the existing
   local reranker on the top 50.
5. Group by document, best chunks first, snippet with highlights.

## What is indexed (one index, per project)
- Text documents and chats (existing chunking: parent / child).
- Imported working copies of PDF / DOCX; OCR text of plans with page and bbox,
  so a hit opens the plan at the spot.
- CSV: header + row text per row; a hit opens the table at the row.
- IFC: per element GlobalId, class, name, property sets as text; a hit opens
  the element in `<ifc-viewer>`.
- Change records (`content_changes` from / to values) and verdict reasons,
  under `in:history`: "when did EI 90 become EI 60?" finds the decision.
- Not indexed: archived apps' code, secrets, settings.
- Index updates on save and import (existing outbox pattern), in background;
  stale-index state shown in the results header.

## Where search lives
- In the line: intent "find / where / which document" routes to a results
  surface; Enter as always.
- Search panel (Mod+F in a document, Mod+Shift+F for the project): keyword
  results update while typing (no AI involved, local index only); semantic
  and rerank run on Enter. This is the one place results appear while typing,
  because no AI acts.
- Bridge / MCP: `search(query, kind?, in_history?, k?)` returns hits with
  `content_id`, version, location (paragraph, page + bbox, row, GlobalId),
  score and snippet.
- Chat and impact checks use the same function for context (RAG), scoped to
  the project.

## Results
- Each hit shows: document, location, snippet with highlights, match type
  (exact / meaning), version if not latest.
- Open = jump to the location in the right surface.
- "Make this an app" on a result list: hands the query and hits to the app
  builder (e.g. all doors with EI 30 → table).
- Searches are not logged in the audit trail unless their results feed an AI
  action; then the query and hit ids are recorded with that action.

# Format engines
One engine per format, registered by file extension in `compare/engines.py`.
Every engine returns `ContentChange` rows plus a render payload; the review
screen picks the fragment by `kind`.

```python
class FormatEngine(Protocol):
    extensions: tuple[str, ...]
    kind: Literal["text", "table", "plan", "model", "image"]
    def diff(self, old: Path, new: Path, ctx: CompareContext) -> EngineResult: ...
    def summarize(self, result: EngineResult) -> str: ...  # plain language, no LLM
```

| Kind  | Extensions          | Engine                                         | Subject / path              |
|-------|---------------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------|
| text  | .md .txt .docx      | existing `compare/` pipeline                   | paragraph id / paragraph n  |
| table | .csv .xlsx          | daff (new dependency); xlsx via openpyxl → CSV | row key / column name       |
| plan  | .pdf                | existing `services/plan_text_diff`             | page n / bbox               |
| model | .ifc                | IfcOpenShell, by GlobalId                      | GlobalId / Pset.Property    |
| image | .png .jpg           | before/after, pixel diff                       | file / region               |

Semantic judgment (embedding bands, LLM tie-break, impact checks) runs on top of
any engine's rows, not inside the engines.

# Review screen
One candidate version against its baseline: aligned changes with diff and
accept / reject / edit. The server holds all state; the browser only displays it.

- `GET /review/{project}/{document}?base=&candidate=` renders `review.html`:
  header (source: user, AI margin action or agent; model; prompt id; reason),
  status bar (open / accepted / rejected counts), list of change fragments,
  "Accept all" / "Reject all".
- Each change fragment has its own HTMX actions:
  - `hx-post=".../changes/{change_id}/accept"`, `.../reject`, `.../edit`
  - `hx-target="closest .change"`, `hx-swap="outerHTML"`
  - The response is the updated fragment plus an out-of-band status bar
    (`hx-swap-oob`).
  - Every verdict is a `verdict` event in the log (actor, reason, baseline
    and candidate ids); the index updates from it.
- Rendering by kind, server-side wherever possible:
  - text: `word_diff_html` output, `<ins>` / `<del>`, kind badge
  - table: HTML `<table>`, changed cells `class="changed"`, old value in `title`
  - plan: `<plan-viewer>` with change pins over the page image
  - model: metadata changes as an HTML table, one row per changed property
    (GlobalId, class, Pset.Property, old, new); geometry as
    `<ifc-diff model-before="....glb" model-after="....glb" model-diff="....glb" changes="...json">`.
    The diff GLB colours each element by category (added, removed, moved,
    reshaped, properties only, unchanged); reshaped elements show added and
    removed volumes from IfcOpenShell booleans, with a before/after overlay as
    fallback. GLBs and change list produced by IfcOpenShell; clicking an
    element emits `ifc-select`, the table scrolls to the matching change
  - image: `<image-compare>` slider
- Concurrency: each fragment carries `data-hash` (sha256 of the candidate).
  If the file changed on disk meanwhile, the server returns 409 with a refreshed
  fragment marked outdated. Nothing is applied against a stale candidate.
- Live updates via SSE: new analysis results, disk changes, newer candidates
  swap in a notice at the top.
- Keyboard (`review-keys.js`): j / k move, a accept, r reject, e edit.
- The screen works without JS except the plan, model and image views.

# Agent rules
`.iterthink/rules.yaml`, inherited workspace → project like all settings.

```yaml
agents:
  default: propose          # propose | direct | deny
  direct:
    - "**/*.md:typos"       # minor text fixes only
  deny:
    - "contracts/**"
  propose_always:
    - "**/*.ifc"
    - "**/*.csv"
deletions: propose
```

- Applies to agents (MCP), ad-hoc apps and AI margin actions alike.
- `propose`: the edit becomes a candidate version in review.
- `direct`: applied without review because the user pre-approved this class of
  change; verdict recorded as "auto · rule <id>", chained, visible in history,
  restorable in one step.
- `deny`: refused, the refusal is logged.
- `direct` is never the default and never applies to deletions, IFC, plans or
  files under `deny`. Rules are enforced in core (`services/agent_rules.py`),
  never only in MCP or the bridge.

# UI style (carried over from 1.x)
- Minimalistic. No explanatory paragraphs in settings: headings, labels,
  placeholders, tooltips (`title`). Exceptions: Privacy Shield and RAG disclaimers.
- Theme tokens from `config.yaml` (`theme.dark`, `theme.light`, `appearance`)
  become CSS custom properties on `:root`; never hardcode colours in templates.
- Monospace for the editor, sans-serif for the UI.
- Diffs: unchanged paragraphs hidden by default; `<ins>` / `<del>` with token
  colours; substantive changes (modified, rephrased with NEW verdict) get a
  high-contrast border.

# Logic rules (carried over)
- Paragraph splitting only through `compare.margin.split_paragraphs`.
- Semantic pipeline order: content hash → alignment → embedding cosine → LLM only
  in the uncertain band. Never call the LLM where hash or cosine decides.
- All LLM calls go through `ai.llm_router`; privacy shield applies to company and
  cloud tiers; token usage is recorded for every remote call.
- LLM and embedding work runs off the request thread (asyncio / worker threads);
  results stream via SSE. A request handler never blocks on a model.

# Audit trail
- The event log is the audit trail. `content_changes` and `audit_events` in
  the index are projections of it for querying.
- Event types cover everything that happened: version created, change
  detected, verdict given, intent classified, app built / kept / discarded,
  export created, agent call, task transitions, rule applied, settings that
  affect AI, claim takeover.
- Every event has actor (`user:<name>` | `agent:<name>` | `app:<slug>` |
  `ai:<prompt id>` | `rule:<id>`), input source (`typed` | `voice` | `file`),
  model, prompt version, timestamp (UTC).
- Seal: each machine log is a hash chain (`hash` = SHA-256 over canonical
  JSON of the event incl. `prev_hash`). Every 100 events, or on close, a
  `checkpoint` event records the current head hashes of all other machine
  logs it has merged, so chains anchor each other and deleting or editing a
  line in any log is detectable.
- Verification: `services/audit.py` verifies all chains and checkpoints on
  open and warns on any break. Audit export writes a standalone verification
  script (stdlib Python only) and a readable report; anyone can verify the
  log without iterthink.
- Logs are never rewritten. Corrections are new events.

# Agents (MCP)
- MCP tools are the bridge registry: the same functions ad-hoc apps call
  (Read, Import, Compare, Judge, Find, Annotate, Model, Table, Language,
  Write via `propose`, Deliver), plus `list_projects` and the task tools
  (`list_tasks`, `claim_task`, `update_task`, `ask`, `complete_task`).
  No MCP-only logic.
- An agent session is bound to one project; every call carries it.
- Agents never write files. `propose` creates a candidate version that
  appears in review like any AI margin action or app output.
- Every agent action is logged with agent name, model and reason.

# Security
- Bind 127.0.0.1 only. Token generated at startup, handed to the page through
  pywebview's JS API, required on every request including SSE.
- CSP for the app shell: same-origin scripts only, no inline scripts.
  Ad-hoc app frames have their own CSP (see Ad-hoc apps); never relax the
  shell's CSP for them.
- Validate every path stays inside the current project (or its workspace for
  workspace settings). Symlinks pointing outside are not followed.
- Secrets stay in the keyring-backed vault; never render them into HTML.

# Prohibited
- No Node, npm, bundler or frontend framework without explicit discussion.
- No business logic in routes, templates or JS. Routes call core and render.
- No cloud dependency by default. Cloud LLMs only when the user configures them.
- No second change model next to `ContentChange`.
- No direct writes to the SQLite index and no log lines written outside
  `services/event_log.py`. No database file inside a project folder.
- No new entity tables. Things are `content` rows, links are
  `content_relations` rows, kinds come from the contract.

# Later (not 2.0)
- History backend: evaluate Jujutsu (`jj`, Git-compatible) with one repo per
  project. Automatic snapshots per save, operation log for undo, conflicts as
  state, Git remotes as thin sync. Fits the storage model: `versions/` and
  `log/` are already plain, append-only files.
  Wrap it in one module (`persistence/vcs.py`); nothing else calls `jj`.
- Sync across devices: implement `sync_outbox` against that remote (e.g.
  self-hosted Forgejo), large files via Git LFS.
- Team review, {yourcompany}os workflow triggers on approval.
